<h2>Leaving the old direction</h2>
<p>
  Sometimes leaving an old direction that doesn't fit anymore is the first
  intentional step toward a new one that does. Sometimes leaving is
  unintentional, beyond our choosing. Either way, a new direction is in front
  of us.
</p>
<p>
  The old direction is well known and familiar. The new one is not. That can be
  scary. It can also be resolutely adventurous and thrilling.
</p>
<p>
  Sometimes it's hard to see the old directions that entangle us, hold us back,
  enslave us, keep us in bondage, prevent our healthiest growth, keep us from
  a more mature version of ourselves.
</p>
<p>
  It's a blessing to see an old direction for how it's not serving us. Even if
  we value it highly. But perceived value does not always equal that which is
  healthiest for us. Our ability to perceive value can be skewed by an old
  direction of thinking we do not even know we have.
</p>
<p>
  That's why it is a blessing to recognize when an old direction that is
  unhealthy, no matter the perceived value, needs to go away. That recognition
  is the first step of the new direction.
</p>

<h2>The new direction has already started</h2>
<figure>
  <img
    src={doorField}
