<h2>Calibration</h2>
<p>
  How do we gauge what is healthiest, and which direction to go?
</p>
<p>
  The answer is simple, and often not easy to accept. The healthiest new
  direction is obvious in proportion to how clearly you know the old one was
  unhealthy. If you do not view the direction you are going as unhealthy, the
  new one will not appeal to you.
</p>
<p>
  When you recognize that the old direction was confining you, restraining you,
  holding you back, stunting your growth, perverting your life, that's when you
  also need to recognize you do not have to keep going that way. Don't tell
  yourself, with an old-directed thought life, that you have no options, no
  alternatives.
</p>
<p>
  Okay, within and of yourself you don't have any options or alternatives. But
  we're not calibrating ourselves by ourselves. There is a direction beyond us
  that can be in us. That is Jesus Christ. He is the direction of highest
  health, fullest maturity.
</p>

<h2>Temporal and eternal directions</h2>
<p>
  There are things in the temporal world we hold onto as our identity because we
  have done them for decades, become experts, or enjoy them so much we cannot
  imagine ourselves without that direction.
</p>
<p>
  That is how it was for me with music. I loved creating music. I spent so much
  of my time, my mind, my energy directed toward it. It took so much bandwidth
  from my life, beyond my awareness even.
</p>
<figure>
  <img
    src={musicStudio}
    alt="A small home music studio left mid-session. An open laptop shows a digital audio workstation. A teal electric guitar stands by a window with closed blinds. Keyboards, a mixer, cables, and a gray chair with a sheepskin seat fill the room."
    width="800"
    height="450"
    decoding="async"
  />
  <figcaption>My Music Studio. An old direction.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>
  It was not until I put music down and found myself in a void, with nothing
  temporal to immediately replace it, that I could see. I could see that I had
  just started a new thing, as unknown as it was at the time. It was the detox
  period. The withdrawal period. I was blessed to go through that with
  excitement instead of reluctance. That excitement was a gift. And that gift
  was received by faith, knowing that my Lord had something better for me.
</p>

<h2>Every letting go</h2>
<p>
  This is not only true of music. God is sovereign (in control) over every
  direction and everything in our lives.
</p>
<p>
  And those in Jesus Christ have nothing to fear and something healthy to gain
  with every new direction. Every letting go can be celebrated, ultimately, in
  Jesus Christ. Even the painful ones.
</p>
<p>Losing a job.</p>
<p>A physical ailment or disability.</p>
<p>Losing a friend or family member.</p>
<p>
  Each one leaves an old direction. And in these examples, we are essentially
  shoved into a new direction. For those in Jesus Christ, even when being
  shoved in a new direction, we can trust Him with it.
</p>
<p>
  My grandma loved Jesus Christ. She knew it was time to leave this world. Her
  health got to a point, and she knew the time was near. Her last lap, so to
  speak, in this phase of life. That was not the end of her. It was the end of
  the old direction and the beginning of the new one.
</p>
<figure>
  <img
    src={grandma}
    alt="A studio portrait of an older woman with short white hair and glasses, wearing a pink jacket embroidered with flowers, seated with her hands folded. A younger man in a dark blue shirt stands beside her with his hand on her shoulder. Both are smiling."
    width="800"
