<p>
  Yes, you can look at it as one long continuous direction too. But for the
  Christian, the transition from this life to the next is leaving the direction of living here
  now, and turning into the new and better direction of living in the presence
  of Jesus Christ.
</p>

<h2>Grief</h2>
<p>
  Just because every new direction in Jesus Christ is one we can celebrate, that
  does not negate grieving. Grieving still has its appropriate place. But it is
  a part of healing, not the place where we stay the rest of our lives.
</p>
<p>
  We do not have to pretend the letting go did not cost something. We also do
  not have to make grief the new direction we live in permanently. If we go too
  far with grief, it can often turn into anger, bitterness, and depression. And
  at that point our new direction regresses into an old unhealthy direction once
  again. So grieve when it's in its proper season. Also remember, whether the
  letting go feels small, life changing, or it's the last one we make here, the
  same thing is true. In Jesus Christ it can be celebrated.
</p>

<h2>Faith</h2>
<p>
  Faith is real. Faith is a blessing. Faith is a gift. Faith is a strength. But
  it has to be faith in that which is healthy. Even better, it has to be faith
  in the One who is completely healthy. The One who leads us in the healthiest
  way. So often we assign faith to unhealthy things and distort the purpose and
  application of faith.
</p>
<p>
  Now faith is the assurance of things hoped for, the conviction of things not
  seen. (Hebrews 11:1).
</p>
<p>
  Faith has to be in that which is real. That which is real is that which is
  true. That which is true is the way to go. That which is true is life.
</p>

<h2>Always new</h2>
<figure>
  <img
    src={sunHorizon}
    alt="A yellow-orange sun sits on the horizon beyond dry brown hills and grassland. Thin pink clouds stretch across a pale blue evening sky."
    width="800"
    height="600"
    decoding="async"
  />
  <figcaption>He is the new direction that is always new.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>
  Jesus is the Way, the Truth, and the Life. No one comes to the Father except
  through Him. (John 14:6). Let's go that direction.
</p>
<p>
  What in your temporal life is holding you back from going that new direction?
  Whatever it is, it's called an idol: something vying for first place, holding
  you back from having Jesus Christ first in your life.
</p>
<p>
  It is a blessing to recognize idols. It is a blessing to be able to let go
  of them.
</p>
<p>
  When we turn from that old direction, we are turning toward the New One.
  Another word for turning to Jesus Christ is repenting. And He is the new
  direction that is always new.
</p>
